  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Immediate life-saving support to people living with HIV/AIDS affected by flooding and mudslide in Fr</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the night of 13th August into the morning of 14th August the greater Freetown area was hit by heavy rainfall, resulting in massive flooding that destroyed houses in several communities and caused the drowning of many people. A massive mudslide occurred at Regent in the early morning hours of 14th of August, destroying over one hundred houses. Over ten low lying communities were flooded on the same morning. As of 19th August 2017, burials of over 300 recovered bodies has been completed. An estimated 1,000 households were affected and around 6,000 displaced (ONS, 19/08/2017). Some have found temporary shelter at schools, churches, mosques and community centres at Regent, Dwazak, Kaningo and Kamayama. While recovery efforts are still ongoing, the humanitarian response efforts are addressing the immediate needs of the affected population.

This 1 week UNAIDS funded project will provide basic items and services for 100 households of PLHIV affected by flooding and mudslide, including food, NFIs, modalities for cash transfer and access to treatment.</narrative>
  </description>
